    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## UXR Recruiting Start\n**Entry point of the recruiting wizard.** Darwin (the research Slack agent) calls this workflow as a tool when someone asks it to recruit research participants.\n\nIt does ONE thing: opens the wizard in the Slack thread by posting step 1 (\"who do you want to talk to?\"). Every later step (filters, sample size, candidates, email, send) is handled by **UXR Interaction Handler**, triggered by the button clicks.\n\n**Inputs from Darwin:** channel_id, thread_ts (the thread where the user asked), user_id.\n**Returns to Darwin:** { success, message } so the agent can confirm or apologise.",
        "height": 352,
        "width": 364,
        "color": 4
      },
      "id": "2f84a908-d91b-4c54-85f3-eb4d87199254",
      "name": "Sticky: What this is",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -416,
        -96
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### 1. Validate the Slack context\nchannel_id must exist and thread_ts must look like a Slack timestamp (digits.digits). Builds the session key `channel_threadts`: one wizard session (= one recruiting campaign) per thread.\n\nInvalid → *Return Invalid Input*: tells Darwin the wizard did not start.",
        "height": 376,
        "width": 296,
        "color": 5
      },
      "id": "e6ecad15-9a72-45f5-a7fe-c85628b20531",
      "name": "Sticky: Validate",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        128,
        -176
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### 2. One recruiting per thread (strict)\n*Get Existing Session* + *Thread Free?* + *Thread Blocked?*: if this thread has EVER had a campaign row (any state), the wizard does NOT start again here: *Return Blocked* tells Darwin to send the user to a new thread. Rule chosen 2026-09-23: a thread is a campaign, full stop; the old thread keeps tracking its bookings.\n\n*Get Slack User* (users.info on the user_id Darwin passes) gives the researcher's email; on error it continues with an empty email (needs the `users:read.email` scope on the bot).\n\nThe campaign row in **UXR Recruiting - Campaigns** (key `session_key` = channel_threadts) is written by *Upsert Session* only AFTER the step-1 card was posted successfully (see step 3), so a failed Slack post never burns the thread.",
        "height": 504,
        "width": 296,
        "color": 5
      },
      "id": "33639e61-6004-4d11-ac41-dbe3c29667a0",
      "name": "Sticky: Session",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        576,
        -608
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### 3. Post step 1 in the thread, then create the campaign row\n*Load Config* → *Build Step 1* renders one card per audience from UXR Config (`audiences`: label, description, pool) with a Choose button (`uxr_audience_<id>`) plus Cancel (`uxr_cancel`). The Handler routes on these action_ids: change an audience id here AND in the Handler, never one side only.\n\n*Check Post Succeeded*: true → *Upsert Session* writes the campaign row (slack_user + slack_user_email, state `audience_select`, empty filters, count 0, updated_at = now) → *Return Result*. On a Slack error the node continues with an `error` field instead of `ok: true`, so the row is NOT created and *Return Error* gives Darwin Slack's error text: the thread stays free for a retry.",
        "height": 508,
        "width": 288,
        "color": 5
      },
      "id": "a0d1c1eb-d21d-460a-a125-95b8efbffc54",
      "name": "Sticky: Post",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        1248,
        -384
      ]
    },
    {
